        <div id="temperature" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Setpoint, Pull-Down Time and How Cold Is Cold Enough
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most home chiller tubs list a minimum setpoint between 32 and 39 degrees Fahrenheit. The Plunge All-In lists 37, the Edge Tub Elite lists 37, and Sun Home lists 32 for its Pro. Those are setpoints under good conditions, not guarantees. A chiller in a hot garage in July works much harder than the same chiller in a cool basement, and the water may not hold the lowest setting in high ambient heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pull-down time is the other number to watch. That is how long the chiller takes to cool a freshly filled tub to the setpoint. Men's Fitness reported the Edge Tub Elite reaching 37 degrees in under three hours with its 1 HP chiller, while the brand lists about five hours for the lower-powered Legacy. Bigger chillers pull down faster and recover faster after each session, which matters if more than one person plunges back to back.
          </p>

          <p>
            You do not need the coldest possible water to get the experience most people are after. Plenty of home plungers settle in the mid 40s to mid 50s. Colder water mostly shortens how long you can comfortably stay in. That makes setpoint range less important than consistency: a tub that reliably holds 45 every morning is more useful than one that can reach 32 but rarely does in your climate.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want both hot and cold, check the heating ceiling. The Plunge All-In lists up to 104 degrees with the optional heater, the Cyber Plunge lists up to 107, and the Edge Tub Elite is listed at 104 or 105 depending on the source. Remember that heating and cooling the same body of water takes time and electricity, so frequent switching is slow and expensive.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sanitation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Keeping the Water Clean
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cold water slows bacterial growth, but it does not stop it. Skin, sweat and whatever is on your feet all end up in the tub, and still water held for weeks needs real sanitation. Chiller tubs typically combine mechanical filtration with ozone, UV or both. Sun Home describes ozone, UV and a 20-micron filter on its Pro, and BlueCube's modular system houses filtration in the separate equipment unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Filtration catches particles; ozone and UV attack microorganisms in the circulating water. Neither replaces basic hygiene. Rinse off before you get in, keep a lid on between sessions, and follow the maker's guidance on water changes and any approved sanitiser. Using chemicals the manufacturer does not approve can damage seals and chillers and may void the warranty.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ice-only barrels have no circulation at all, so the water is only as clean as the last time you changed it. That usually means draining and refilling every few sessions, which is easy with a barrel's drain spigot but adds water use and time. If you plan to leave water in for weeks, a circulating chiller with filtration is close to essential.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan for filter replacements and ozone or UV components as ongoing costs. Check what the maker recommends and what replacement parts cost before you buy, because a tub with cheap, easily available filters is easier to keep running than one with proprietary parts that are often out of stock.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Power, Placement and Weight
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most home chiller tubs run on standard 120-volt power, but the amperage matters. Rogue's guide says the Plunge All-In uses a 15-amp household circuit while a Plunge comparison page lists 20 amps; the Cyber Plunge recommends a dedicated 15-amp outlet; BlueCube calls for a 120-volt, 20-amp dedicated outlet. Sharing a circuit with a freezer or space heater invites nuisance trips. Have an electrician confirm the circuit, and use a GFCI-protected outlet for anything near water, as electrical codes generally require.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight is the other planning issue. Water weighs about 8.3 pounds per gallon, so a 105-gallon tub holds roughly 870 pounds of water before the tub and the person. Plunge lists the All-In at about 1,150 pounds filled, and Rogue lists the Ice Barrel 300 at about 700. On a ground-floor slab that is fine. On a deck or an upper floor, ask a contractor whether the structure can carry that load, as you would for a hot tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            Outdoors, think about sun, rain and freezing. Direct summer sun makes a chiller work harder and can push the water above setpoint; shade or a garage helps. In winter, check whether the maker allows outdoor operation below freezing and how to protect the plumbing. A level, well-drained pad matters too. If you are pouring a new slab for a tub, our guide to <a href="/concrete-patio-cost/">concrete patio costs</a> covers pricing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chillers make noise and shed heat. If the tub is indoors, give the chiller ventilation and keep it away from bedrooms. A modular system like BlueCube's, which lets the equipment sit up to 60 feet away, solves that at a price. Humidity is also worth watching in enclosed rooms; a <a href="/best-hygrometer/">hygrometer</a> tells you if a cold tub and a warm room are producing condensation problems.
          </p>
        </div>

