            7. What a Cold Plunge Really Costs to Own
          </h2>

          <p>
            Purchase price is only the start. An all-in-one chiller tub in this guide costs several thousand dollars up front, and premium or commercial units cost far more. A chiller-ready barrel costs less than two thousand dollars, but a separate chiller adds to that. Inflatable tubs are cheaper still, but they are less durable and less insulated.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electricity depends on the chiller, the setpoint and the room. A chiller holding cold water in a warm garage runs much more than one in a cool basement. Rather than trust a single estimate, check the chiller's rated power, look at your electricity rate, and expect higher bills in summer. A good lid makes a real difference, because most of the heat gain comes through the open surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ice is the hidden cost of the cheaper route. If each session takes a couple of bags of ice, frequent plunging adds up to hundreds of dollars over a year, plus the time spent buying it. That is the calculation that pushes most regular plungers to a chiller eventually, and it is why we suggest a chiller-ready barrel if you are unsure.
          </p>

